# Independent review

After an authorized push succeeds, delegate a review of the pushed code to a
fresh-context background agent. Keep the main session free for other work and
report the result when it arrives. This skill grants no permission to push, fix
code, post comments, or send notifications outside the current conversation.

## Record the push scope

Before pushing, record the remote, each destination ref, its existing full commit
SHA, and the full commit SHA intended for that ref. Resolve the actual refspecs;
do not assume the current branch, upstream, or `HEAD` is what will be pushed.
Use an authoritative remote ref observation or push receipt, not a potentially
stale remote-tracking ref. Keep this bookkeeping short; do not start a review or
wait for one before pushing.

After the push, check its per-ref outcome. Review only successfully updated refs,
including successful updates in a partially failed push. Skip failed, unchanged,
and deleted refs, and report why a deletion has no code snapshot to review.

- Existing branch: compare the recorded old commit to the pushed new commit.
  Include every commit introduced by the update, not just the last commit.
- New branch: record that the old ref was absent. Choose a justified baseline
  from the intended target branch, pin its SHA, and explain that choice in the
  report. For an empty remote or a standalone orphan history with no target
  baseline, use an explicit empty-tree baseline and inspect all reachable commits.
  Do not invent an old SHA or silently review only the latest commit.
- Force update: compare the old and new trees directly; include the newly
  reachable commits. Do not replace the old SHA with a merge-base.
- Multiple refs: track each update separately. Review commit-bearing tags by
  their peeled commit SHAs; explain unsupported non-commit refs.

If scope was not captured, cannot be recovered reliably, or changed concurrently
on the remote, report `incomplete` for that ref. Do not delay or retry the push to
obtain a review. Never infer its old SHA from the remote after the update.

## Prepare an isolated snapshot

Read [scripts/prepare_review.py](scripts/prepare_review.py) for its CLI, then run
it once per review scope after push success. It requires Python 3.11+ and Git:

```text
python3 <skill-directory>/scripts/prepare_review.py --repo <source-repository> --base <full-base-sha> --head <full-pushed-sha>
```

For a new ref with no target baseline, replace `--base <sha>` with `--root`. Never
use root mode to hide a missing old SHA for an existing ref.

The script creates a standalone temporary Git repository at the pinned head,
retains the base for comparison, and prints JSON with `directory`, `base_sha`,
`head_sha`, and `baseline_kind` (`commit` or `empty-tree`). Root mode creates the
empty tree in the snapshot's object format. The helper accepts a repository root,
subdirectory, linked worktree, or bare repository. Its Git commands exclude
inherited Git environment and global/system settings; external content filters
do not run during checkout. It neither pushes nor launches a model. Add remote/ref
identity and baseline provenance to the task message; its output alone is not
proof of a successful push. Missing commits or setup failure mean `incomplete`.

Keep the original working tree untouched, even if it is dirty. Retain the
snapshot until the reviewer stops. Then remove only the temporary directory
created by the script; never remove a user workspace or clean an active review.
Do not fetch submodules or Git LFS content implicitly. If missing content prevents
reviewing affected behavior, state the gap and return `incomplete`.

## Start a fresh background reviewer

Read [references/reviewer.md](references/reviewer.md). Give the reviewer the path
to that reference, the snapshot directory, push identity, pinned SHAs, original
requirements and constraints, and relevant repository rules/documents. Preserve
the user's requirements without adding the author's interpretation. The reviewer
must read project instructions from the pinned snapshot.

Do not pass the parent transcript, implementation plan, reasoning, self-review,
prior conclusions, or claims that tests passed. Do not reuse an agent or memory
from implementation or a previous review. Let the reviewer collect test evidence.
Do not substitute a fork of the author conversation for a fresh context.

Verify that the host's existing permissions allow the reviewer to read and run
commands in the temporary snapshot. Do not change permissions to enable access.
If access is denied, return `incomplete`; never fall back to the author's checkout.

